---
title: "What nut?"
subtitle: "How much life nuts buy, and whether the kind matters"
author:
  - name: Max Ghenis
    email: max@maxghenis.com
    url: https://www.maxghenis.com
date: 2026-09-23
abstract: |
  Cohort studies find that people who eat nuts die at lower rates, and that the association stops growing by about 15 grams a day. Randomized trials find that tree nuts lower LDL cholesterol, with no significant difference among the kinds tested. I turn both into remaining life expectancy with the 2023 US life tables. No trial has measured how much of the cohort association nuts cause, so I report scenarios that run from randomized evidence alone to the cohort association at face value. For a 40-year-old man who eats no nuts, 15 grams a day adds {{m40_ldl_range}} days through the LDL pathway alone, {{m40_cal}} days after discounting the association by the typical gap between nutrition trials and cohorts, and {{m40_face}} days, about {{m40_face_years}} years, at face value. A 40-year-old woman gains {{f40_ldl_range}} days through the LDL pathway, {{f40_cal}} after the discount and {{f40_face}} at face value. The first 5 grams account for {{any_share}} of the face-value gain, and that step, from no nuts to some, is where differences between people who eat nuts and people who do not would show most. The evidence cannot rank walnuts, almonds, pistachios, pecans and hazelnuts; peanuts, cashews and macadamias lack trial evidence that they lower LDL; and macadamias cost {{price_ratio}} times as much per gram as peanuts.
---

# The question {#sec-question}

For breakfast I eat a pudding adapted from Bryan Johnson's recipe, with three tablespoons of nuts blended in: walnuts and macadamias from his recipe, almonds and hazelnuts added. I wanted to know what the nuts buy and whether the kinds matter.

How much remaining life does eating nuts buy a US adult of a given age and sex who starts now and keeps going? How much do the next grams add for someone who already eats some? And does the kind of nut change the answer once price counts?

I measure each answer as the change in remaining life expectancy, in undiscounted days or years, when someone who eats $B$ grams of nuts a day adds Δ grams at age $a_0$ and keeps eating them. Many lipid trials fed nuts in place of other foods at equal calories, and many cohort analyses adjust for total energy intake, so the estimates describe nuts eaten in place of other calories; adding nuts on top of an unchanged diet is a different intervention. Cost per life-year discounts both costs and life-years at {{discount_pct}}.

The hard part is causation. People who eat nuts differ from people who do not, and no trial has randomized nuts alone against no nuts and counted deaths. So I report six scenarios: two built only from randomized evidence on LDL cholesterol, two calibrations of the cohort association against how nutrition cohorts have compared with trials, the association restricted to cardiovascular deaths, and the association at face value. @tbl-reference shows every scenario; later results name the ones they use.

# What the evidence says {#sec-evidence}

## Cohorts: the association and its shape {#sec-cohorts}

Aune and colleagues' 2016 dose-response meta-analysis of prospective cohorts, the source of the relative risk Fadnes and colleagues used, pooled 15 studies with 85,870 deaths among 819,448 people and found a relative risk of death of {{ev_aune_allcause}} per 28 grams a day of tree nuts and peanuts [@aune2016nut]. Two features of that estimate argue for caution. Studies with less than ten years of follow-up found {{ev_aune_fu_short}}, and those with ten or more found {{ev_aune_fu_long}}. If nuts took years to act, the short studies should show less, not more; a stronger association early on is what illness that makes people stop eating nuts would produce. Smaller studies also reported stronger associations (Egger's test, P = 0.02); without the five smallest, the estimate is {{ev_aune_large}}. A 2022 umbrella review reports Aune's estimate unchanged and shares two of its authors [@balakrishna2022umbrella], and a 2026 meta-analysis found {{ev_liu2026}} for the highest against the lowest intake [@liu2026nutmeta].

The linear number hides the shape. On the study's own nonlinear curve, the relative risk falls to {{aune_rr_5g}} at 5 grams a day, {{aune_rr_10g}} at 10 and {{aune_rr_min}} at {{aune_min_g}}, then drifts back to {{aune_rr_28g}} at 28 grams (@fig-dose-curves). The authors report no further reduction above 15 to 20 grams a day. The Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation's (IHME) Burden of Proof analysis of nuts and seeds against ischemic heart disease flattens earlier still: its mean relative risk reaches {{bop_rr_flat}} by about {{bop_flat_g}} grams a day and stays there, and IHME rates the association {{bop_stars}} stars out of five, its grade for weak evidence [@ihmebop2026nutsihd], by the method of @zheng2022bop.

![Relative risk of death by grams of nuts a day. Points are Aune and colleagues' printed nonlinear estimates for all-cause mortality with 95% confidence intervals; the model holds that curve at its lowest value once reached. The dashed line is the linear per-28 g estimate with a plateau, a sensitivity. The green line and band are IHME's mean and uncertainty interval for nuts and seeds against ischemic heart disease, a different exposure and outcome.](figures/dose_curves.png){#fig-dose-curves}

The association also appears where some of the usual confounders run the other way. In the Golestan cohort in northeastern Iran, nut eaters smoked more, drank more, exercised less and weighed more than people who ate none, but they were also younger, wealthier, more urban and better educated, and they had a hazard ratio for death of {{ev_golestan}} at three or more servings a week [@eslamparast2017golestan]. Among low-income Black and white adults in the US South, the top fifth of nut and peanut butter intake, 18 grams a day or more, had a hazard ratio of {{ev_luu_sccs}} [@luu2015nut]. The same paper's Shanghai cohorts found {{ev_luu_shanghai}} for the top fifth of peanut intake. But the second fifth, {{luu_q2_range}} grams a day, already had {{ev_luu_shanghai_q2}}, and the association did not deepen above it. It is hard to see how a fraction of a peanut a day could cut deaths by a fifth; what differs is whether people eat any nuts at all.

Cohorts barely separate the kinds of nut. In the Nurses' Health and Health Professionals Follow-up studies, peanuts twice a week or more went with a hazard ratio for incident cardiovascular disease of {{ev_gf_peanut}}, tree nuts {{ev_gf_treenut}}, and walnuts once a week or more {{ev_gf_walnut}} [@guaschferre2017nut]. Peanut butter showed no association there or in the Netherlands Cohort Study [@vandenbrandt2015nlcs]. In the US Southern cohort its top fifth had a hazard ratio of {{ev_luu_pb}}, with no trend across fifths [@luu2015nut].

Mendelian randomization, which uses gene variants that shift nut intake as a natural experiment, adds little. One study, with genetic predictors of nut intake from UK Biobank and outcomes from FinnGen, found no effect of any nut exposure on any cardiovascular outcome that survived its correction for multiple testing. Too few variants reached the usual significance threshold, so its instruments were chosen at a relaxed one [@wang2025mr]. Salted or roasted peanuts gave its only suggestive signal, an odds ratio for ischemic heart disease of {{ev_wang_salted}}.

## Trials: LDL, and one trial with events {#sec-trials}

Across 61 controlled trials that fed a median of {{delgobbo_dose_all}} grams a day for a median of {{delgobbo_weeks_all}} weeks, the change in LDL cholesterol, scaled to an ounce (28.4 grams) of tree nuts a day, was {{ev_delgobbo_ldl_all}} mg/dL. Restricted to randomized trials, it was {{ev_delgobbo_ldl_rct}} mg/dL for LDL and {{ev_delgobbo_apob_rct}} mg/dL for apolipoprotein B. The authors found no significant difference by type of tree nut, though two or fewer trials tested cashews, pecans or Brazil nuts [@delgobbo2015nuts]. That meta-analysis excluded peanuts. Meta-analyses of single nuts find LDL reductions for walnuts, almonds, pistachios, pecans and hazelnuts, and no significant effect for cashews, peanuts or, in one trial, macadamias (@tbl-nut-ldl).

{{table:nut_ldl}}

: LDL cholesterol change in controlled trials, by nut (mg/dL; hazelnut converted from mmol/L). The pooled tree-nut row is per 28.4 grams a day; each nut row is a meta-analysis (macadamia: one trial) at the doses its trials fed, against differing comparators. The pooled review was funded by the International Tree Nut Council, the walnut and almond analyses by those nuts' industry boards, and the macadamia trial by Hort Innovation, an Australian horticulture industry body. {#tbl-nut-ldl}

One trial counted cardiovascular events. PREDIMED assigned Spanish adults at high cardiovascular risk, most of them individually at random, to a Mediterranean diet with {{predimed_nut_g}} grams a day of mixed nuts (half walnuts, a quarter each hazelnuts and almonds), a Mediterranean diet with extra-virgin olive oil, or advice to cut fat [@estruch2018predimed]. Its first report was withdrawn after some participants were found to have been assigned without randomization, and the 2018 reanalysis accounts for them. The nut arm's hazard ratio for major cardiovascular events was {{ev_predimed_nuts}}; the olive-oil arm's was {{ev_predimed_evoo}}. The benefit came mostly from stroke, {{ev_predimed_stroke}}. For death from any cause, the outcome this paper models, the nut arm's hazard ratio was {{ev_predimed_death}}, and for cardiovascular death {{ev_predimed_cvdeath}}. The trial cannot separate the nuts from the rest of the diet. Over its median {{predimed_years}} years of follow-up, the cohort curve with a ten-year phase-in implies a hazard ratio near {{predimed_phased_hr}}, inside the trial's interval for death; the curve's full effect, {{aune_rr_min}}, lies below it. In the same participants, those who ate nuts more than three times a week at baseline had a hazard ratio for death of {{ev_gf2013}} against those who rarely or never did [@guaschferre2013predimed], an observational association the randomized comparison did not reproduce.

## How much of the association is causal {#sec-causal}

Randomized evidence gives one estimate through the LDL pathway. The Cholesterol Treatment Trialists pooled randomized statin trials and estimated what each mmol/L of LDL lowering does to deaths: {{ev_ctt_chd}} for coronary deaths and {{ev_ctt_allcause}} for deaths from any cause [@ctt2010ldl]. Multiplying the nut trials' randomized LDL effect by those slopes gives what randomized evidence supports if nuts act through LDL the way statins do per mmol/L. Trials that lowered LDL by diet and other non-statin means found about the same benefit per mmol/L as statins, {{ev_silverman}} for major vascular events [@silverman2016ldl]. The estimate is not a strict floor. It leaves out every other pathway, and statin trials lasted about five years, while lower LDL sustained for decades appears to do more per mmol/L [@ference2017ldl]. But its LDL effect comes from randomized trials that fed a median of {{delgobbo_dose_rct}} grams a day for {{delgobbo_weeks_rct}} weeks, and the authors found larger effects at 60 grams a day or more, so scaling down to 15 grams may overstate it. The one two-year trial, of walnuts at 30 to 60 grams a day in older adults, found {{ev_waha}} mg/dL [@rajaram2021waha], against the {{waha_implied}} mg/dL the short trials imply at 45 grams.

Calibration scales the cohort association by how nutrition cohorts have compared with trials of the same question. Schwingshackl and colleagues matched 71 pairs of meta-analyses, one of trials and one of cohorts asking the same diet-disease question, and divided the trials' relative risk by the cohorts' [@schwingshackl2021agreement]. Across all 71 pairs the ratio was {{ev_schw_overall}}, with a 95% prediction interval of {{schw_overall_pi}}. For all-cause mortality, a stratum in which 11 of its 15 pairs test supplements, it was {{ev_schw_allcause}}. When both designs measured dietary intake, it was {{ev_schw_intake}}, but those 23 pairs include pregnancy outcomes and colorectal adenomas, and single pairs range from 0.63 to 3.07. The authors caution that the ratio shows a difference between bodies of evidence, and that its direction depends on the direction of the underlying effects. No pair concerns nuts. The six nearest, three on ALA and three on the Mediterranean diet, pool to {{schw_analog}}, but they rest on two sources of trial evidence, and the Mediterranean-diet trial is PREDIMED, whose nut arm is half of it.

I apply a ratio by asking what share $c$ of the cohort association survives if trials of nuts would differ from the cohorts by that ratio:

$$
c = \frac{\ln(RR_{28} \cdot RRR)}{\ln RR_{28}},
$$ {#eq-calibration}

where $RR_{28}$ is Aune's linear estimate per 28 grams, {{rr28_linear}}, and $RRR$ is the ratio of risk ratios. Each draw of $RRR$ from its prediction interval is paired with the same draw of $RR_{28}$ that scales the curve. The main calibration uses all 71 pairs and keeps {{c_cal_point}} of the association. The mortality calibration uses the all-cause-mortality pairs and keeps {{c_calmort_point}}. Both prediction intervals include ratios large enough to reverse the association, so in {{harm_share_main}} of the main calibration's draws and {{harm_share_mort}} of the mortality calibration's, nuts do harm. The five calibrations in @tbl-calibration keep between {{c_min_point}} and {{c_max_point}}, and the main one sits in the middle. Anchoring $c$ on the modeled curve's relative risk at 28 grams, {{rr28_curve}}, instead of the linear estimate changes each calibration, the mortality calibration most, as the table's last columns show.

{{table:calibration}}

: Calibrations of the cohort association against Schwingshackl and colleagues' trial-cohort pairs, and days of remaining life expectancy gained by a 40-year-old man from 15 grams a day with no prior nuts, mean and 80% interval. The share kept is $c$ at the point estimates. {#tbl-calibration}

# Model {#sec-model}

The baseline is the 2023 US life table for each sex [@arias2025lifetables]. From starting age $a_0$, intake moves from $B$ to $B + \Delta$ grams a day and stays there. For the cohort-curve scenarios, the hazard of death at each later age $a$ is multiplied by

$$
M(a) = \exp\bigl(\varphi(a)\, c\, [f(B + \Delta) - f(B)]\bigr),
$$ {#eq-multiplier}

where $f(g)$ is the log relative risk at intake $g$, $c$ is the share of the association the scenario treats as causal, and $\varphi(a)$ ramps linearly from zero at $a_0$ to one {{phase_in_years}} years later, the convention Fadnes and colleagues used [@fadnes2022food]. Remaining life expectancy with and without the change comes from the life table with the mid-year convention; the model reproduces the table's own life expectancy within {{le_max_gap_days}} at every age.

The dose curve $f$ is Aune's nonlinear all-cause curve, held at its lowest relative risk once reached (@fig-dose-curves), so on the curve itself more nuts never raise risk; only a calibration draw that reverses the association makes them harmful. Because $f$ flattens, the gain from Δ grams depends on $B$: ten grams added to ten buy less than ten grams added to none. The curve is steepest over its first 5 grams, about {{almonds_5g}} almonds, which account for {{any_share}} of the face-value gain at 15 grams. That step is the contrast between people who eat nuts and people who eat none, where confounding would concentrate.

The LDL-pathway and cardiovascular-only scenarios change the hazard differently. The LDL pathway's full-effect log multiplier is $\beta = \ln S$ times the LDL reduction in mmol/L, where $S$ is the statin trialists' rate ratio per mmol/L and the reduction is Del Gobbo's randomized per-28.4-gram change, scaled linearly to Δ and divided by {{ldl_conv}}. With the all-cause slope it multiplies every death's hazard by $\exp(\varphi(a)\beta)$. The coronary version and the cardiovascular-only scenario act only on one cause's share $p(a)$ of deaths,

$$
M(a) = 1 - p(a) + p(a)\exp\bigl(\varphi(a)\,\beta\bigr),
$$ {#eq-cause}

where $p(a)$ is the 2023 share of coronary (ICD-10 I20–I25) or all cardiovascular (I00–I99) deaths for the person's sex and ten-year age group [@nchsmcd2023], and $\beta$ is the LDL multiplier or, for the cardiovascular-only scenario, Aune's cardiovascular-mortality curve at face value.

Each of {{mc_draws}} Monte Carlo draws samples the cohort curve's scale (the whole curve scaled by a lognormal draw of Aune's linear per-28 g estimate, not by the pointwise intervals in @fig-dose-curves; the cardiovascular curve likewise by its own per-28 g estimate), the calibration ratios (from their prediction intervals), the LDL effect, the statin slopes and the ALA slope, with common random numbers across scenarios. The face-value intervals reflect uncertainty in the pooled mean only, not the heterogeneity between studies (I² = 66%), so they are narrower than the calibrated scenarios' by construction. Intervals are 80% unless noted.

The main model gives every nut the same effect per gram: trials find no significant difference in LDL lowering among tree nuts, and cohorts find peanuts and tree nuts about equal. One sensitivity adds a channel for alpha-linolenic acid (ALA), the plant omega-3 that walnuts have and other nuts mostly lack, using the cohort estimate of {{ev_naghshi}} per gram a day and crediting only ALA inside the range those cohorts observed, {{ala_lo}} to {{ala_hi}} grams a day [@naghshi2021ala]. Smaller studies pull that estimate down; a fixed-effect model, which weights studies by precision, gives {{ev_naghshi_fixed}}. US men eat {{us_ala_m}} grams of ALA a day and women {{us_ala_f}}, counting all 18:3 fatty acids [@usdawweia1720].

Other sensitivities measure the curve from 5 grams, so the step from none to some counts for nothing; rescale it to the cohorts with ten or more years of follow-up and to the estimate without the smallest studies; replace it with the linear per-28 g estimate with a plateau; leave deaths from external causes (ICD-10 V01–Y89), {{ext_share_m35}} of deaths of men aged 35 to 44 in 2023, out of the multiplier; halve the log relative risk between ages {{att_from}} and {{att_to}}, a modeling choice for an association otherwise held constant to age 100; change the phase-in; use the 2021 life tables; and use all 61 LDL trials, randomized or not.

With Fadnes and colleagues' inputs for US adults starting at 20 (zero to 25 grams a day, relative risk {{fadnes_rr}}, a ten-year phase-in), the life-table engine gives {{repro_m20}} years for men against their {{fadnes_m20}}, and {{repro_f20}} for women against their {{fadnes_f20}} [@fadnes2022food]. Starting at 80 the match is looser: {{repro_f80}} years for women against their {{fadnes_f80}}. Their baseline was the Global Burden of Disease's 2019 US death rates; this model uses the 2023 life tables. The check covers the life table and the phase-in, not the dose curve or the calibration.

# Results {#sec-results}

## How much {#sec-how-much}

On the cohort curve, 15 and 28 grams a day buy the same for a 40-year-old, because the curve reaches its lowest point at 15 grams; only the LDL pathway keeps growing with dose (@tbl-reference).

{{table:reference}}

: Days of remaining life expectancy gained from starting to eat nuts at age 40 with no prior nut intake, by scenario, sex and amount. Mean and 80% interval. {#tbl-reference}

The scenarios span two orders of magnitude. At 15 grams a day, a 40-year-old man gains {{m40_ldl_range}} days through the LDL pathway, {{m40_calmort}} at the mortality calibration, {{m40_cal}} at the main calibration and {{m40_face}} at face value (@fig-scenarios). Randomized trials support about {{m40_ldl_weeks}} weeks of life through LDL; cohorts observe about {{m40_face_years}} years that nobody has randomized. Face value scales roughly in proportion to $c$: at $c$ of {{dial_lo}} and {{dial_mid}}, the man gains {{m40_dial_lo}} and {{m40_dial_mid}} days.

![Days of remaining life expectancy gained from 15 grams of nuts a day starting at age 40 with no prior nuts, by scenario and sex. Dots are means, thick lines 80% intervals and thin lines 95%.](figures/scenarios_reference.png){#fig-scenarios}

Starting later buys less. At the main calibration, a man who starts at {{age_young}} gains {{m_young_cal}} days and one who starts at {{age_old}} gains {{m_old_cal}}; at face value, {{m_young_face}} and {{m_old_face}} (@fig-by-age).

![Days of remaining life expectancy gained from 15 grams of nuts a day, by starting age, women (left) and men (right). Lines are means for the main calibration and face value; the blue band is the main calibration's 80% interval; the green band spans the LDL pathway from coronary deaths only to all deaths (means).](figures/days_by_age.png){#fig-by-age}

Most of the modeled gain arrives with the first grams. For a 40-year-old man at face value, 5 grams a day buys {{share_5g}} of what 15 grams buys, and 10 grams {{share_10g}} (@fig-dose).

![Days of remaining life expectancy gained at age 40 by grams of nuts a day added to none, women (left) and men (right). Lines and bands as in @fig-by-age.](figures/days_by_dose.png){#fig-dose}

## How much more {#sec-how-much-more}

Because the curve flattens by 15 grams, the next grams are worth less to someone who already eats nuts. For a 40-year-old man, ten more grams a day add {{marg_cal_b0}}, {{marg_cal_b10}} and {{marg_cal_b20}} days at the main calibration, starting from none, 10 and 20 grams; at face value, {{marg_face_b0}}, {{marg_face_b10}} and {{marg_face_b20}} (@fig-marginal). From 20 grams on, only the LDL pathway still adds days, {{marg_ldl_chd}} to {{marg_ldl_all}} at any starting point, because the model scales the trials' LDL effect linearly with dose while the cohort curve is flat. The average US adult eats about {{us_nuts_g}} grams of nuts and seeds on a given day [@usdafped1720; @usdafped1718method], a mean that counts the many people who ate none that day.

![Days of remaining life expectancy from ten more grams of nuts a day at age 40, by grams already eaten, women (left) and men (right). Lines and bands as in @fig-by-age.](figures/marginal_by_background.png){#fig-marginal}

Seeds complicate the background. Aune's exposure is tree nuts and peanuts; IHME's is nuts and seeds together. I eat a tablespoon of ground flax and two of chia every morning, so on IHME's curve I may already sit on the flat part before counting the nuts in my pudding.

## Which nut {#sec-which-nut}

The evidence does not rank the tree nuts. Their LDL effects at the doses trialed overlap (@tbl-nut-ldl), their cohort associations overlap, and no trial has compared hard outcomes between them. Walnuts have the most nut-specific cohort evidence, but the top walnut categories in those cohorts ate a median of only 5 to 7 grams a day [@guaschferre2017nut]. Peanuts match tree nuts in cohorts, but lipid trials have not shown that peanuts lower LDL, and peanut butter shows no consistent association. Cashews and macadamias have the weakest trial evidence.

Price separates them. On {{price_date}}, the median price per kilogram across {{sellers_min}} to {{sellers_max}} warehouse, big-box and specialty sellers per nut ran from {{cheapest_price}} for {{cheapest_nut}} to {{priciest_price}} for {{priciest_nut}}. Because the main model gives every nut the same benefit per gram, cost per life-year follows price. For a 40-year-old man eating 15 grams a day it runs from {{cpl_cheapest}} for {{cheapest_nut}} to {{cpl_priciest}} for {{priciest_nut}} at the main calibration, and from {{cpl_cheapest_face}} to {{cpl_priciest_face}} at face value (@fig-cost). At 28 grams the extra 13 grams cost more and, on the cohort curve, buy nothing. Peanuts come out cheapest on the strength of their cohort association; their lipid trials found no significant LDL effect.

![Cost per life-year gained from 15 grams of nuts a day starting at age 40, by nut and sex, at the main calibration (filled) and face value (hollow), with costs and life-years discounted at {{discount_pct}}. Each nut uses the median of its sellers' prices; labels give the cost of a year's supply.](figures/cost_per_life_year.png){#fig-cost}

ALA is the one channel that could favor walnuts. For a man at the US average, 15 grams of walnuts adds {{walnut_ala}} grams of ALA, of which {{walnut_ala_counted_m}} falls inside the range cohorts observed. Crediting it adds {{ala_premium_m}} days at face value on top of the effect every nut gets, or {{ala_premium_m_fixed}} with the fixed-effect estimate; the larger figure is an upper bound. The sensitivity assumes ALA's cohort association is causal and separate from the nut association, though walnut eaters sit inside the cohorts behind both. For someone whose seeds already supply {{ala_high_bg}} grams of ALA a day, walnut ALA adds {{ala_premium_seeds}}. Randomized trials of ALA found little or no difference in deaths, from five trials with 459 deaths: a risk ratio of {{ev_abdelhamid}} [@abdelhamid2020omega3].

Of the two nuts my pudding takes from Johnson's recipe, walnuts are the one nut ALA could favor, and macadamias cost the most per life-year and have a trial LDL estimate whose interval crosses zero.

## Sensitivity {#sec-sensitivity}

Beyond the causal question (@tbl-reference, @tbl-calibration), treating the step from none to 5 grams as confounded moves the answer most: measuring the curve from 5 grams keeps {{from_any_share}} of the face-value gain. Restricting the association to cardiovascular deaths keeps {{cvd_only_share}}, rescaling it to the cohorts followed ten years or more keeps {{fu10_share}}, and halving the relative risk in old age keeps {{att_share}}. The linear per-28 g estimate with a plateau raises it by {{plateau_add}}. At 15 grams the curve as printed matches the modeled curve; at 28 grams, where the printed curve turns up, it gives {{pchip28_face}} days at face value against {{main28_face}}. The 2021 life tables, from a year when COVID-19 raised death rates, add {{baseline_2021_add}} (@tbl-sensitivity).

{{table:sensitivity}}

: Days of remaining life expectancy gained, 40-year-old man, 0 to 15 grams a day, mean and 80% interval. Each row below the first changes one choice from the main case; a dash marks a scenario the choice does not change. {#tbl-sensitivity}

# What would change the answer {#sec-flip}

Three kinds of evidence would narrow the scenarios. A randomized trial of nuts alone against no nuts, large enough to count deaths, would replace the calibration with a measurement. Studies of people who change what they eat come closest now: in the Nurses' Health and Health Professionals cohorts, each half serving a day more over four years went with a relative risk of cardiovascular disease of {{ev_liu2020_change}}, and people who went from no nuts to half a serving a day or more had {{ev_liu2020_start}}, with each cohort's interval crossing one [@liu2020nutchange]. Mendelian randomization with strong genetic instruments for nut intake would test the cohort association without its confounders. A lipid trial comparing peanuts or cashews head to head with tree nuts at matched doses would say whether the kind matters for the pathway trials can measure.

The dose is less settled than it looks. Every cohort source puts most of the association below 20 grams a day, but that shape is also what a confounded contrast between eaters and non-eaters would produce; the LDL pathway, the part trials support, grows with dose.

# Practical notes {#sec-practical}

Walnuts have {{walnut_pufa}} grams of polyunsaturated fat per 100 grams, the most of the nine nuts in this paper [@usdasrlegacy2018]. In ground walnut sealed at 20 °C for ten months, linolenic acid tended to fall and polyphenols were lower under oxygen than under nitrogen, though most oxidation products did not differ significantly [@vidrih2012walnut]. Milled flaxseed stored {{flax_days}} days at room temperature showed no significant rise in peroxides [@malcolmson2000flax]. Neither study tested whole nuts or cold storage, or sampled at two weeks. Roasting at high temperatures raised malondialdehyde, a marker of lipid oxidation, up to {{roast_mda}}-fold in walnuts, and the authors recommend low to medium temperatures [@schlormann2015roasting].

Whole chia may deliver less ALA than milled chia. In a ten-week trial of 25 grams a day in overweight postmenopausal women, milled chia raised plasma ALA by {{chia_milled}}% and whole chia did not change it significantly [@nieman2012chia]; the trial measured blood ALA, not absorption.

Brazil nuts are a selenium supplement more than a nut. A single kernel averages about {{brazil_se_per_nut}} micrograms of selenium, and USDA's samples range twentyfold, from 136 to 2,740 micrograms per 100 grams [@usdafdc170569]. The European Food Safety Authority's upper limit for adults is {{efsa_se}} micrograms a day [@efsa2023selenium]; the US limit is {{nasem_se}} [@iom2000dri]. In a secondary analysis of a randomized trial in US dermatology patients, 200 micrograms a day of selenium went with more type 2 diabetes, a hazard ratio of {{ev_npc}} [@stranges2007selenium]; the larger SELECT trial found a smaller, nonsignificant increase, {{ev_select}} [@lippman2009select]. I leave Brazil nuts out of the model.

Nuts add calories: {{kcal_walnut}} kilocalories in 28 grams of walnuts, {{kcal_almond}} in almonds [@usdasrlegacy2018]. The estimates here assume they replace other calories (@sec-question).

# Limitations {#sec-limitations}

Face value assumes the cohort association is causal, and its steepest part is the contrast between people who eat any nuts and people who eat none; studies with shorter follow-up found stronger associations, as reverse causation would produce. No calibration pair concerns nuts, and how a ratio of risk ratios maps onto a dose curve is a choice that moves the mortality calibration from {{m40_calmort}} to {{m40_calmort_curve}} days. The LDL pathway assumes nuts act as statins do per mmol/L and rests on short trials at high doses; it could be wrong in either direction. The relative risk is held constant to age 100 and applied to every cause of death. The estimates are for nuts eaten in place of other calories. Much of the lipid evidence was funded by nut industry bodies. The baseline applies one year of US death rates to a person's whole remaining life. The model counts deaths, not quality of life. Prices come from one day, {{price_date}}, from stores in Washington, DC and online sellers.

# Reproducing this paper {#sec-reproduce}

A pipeline computes every model result in this paper from the evidence rows in @tbl-evidence and hash-checked data files and writes it into the text; study sizes and trial counts quoted from sources are copied from the same rows. The rows, data fetchers, model, tests and a citation check that resolves every DOI and matches its title and first author are at [github.com/MaxGhenis/whatnut](https://github.com/MaxGhenis/whatnut/tree/{{version}}).

